L. FINAL SCIENTIFIC QUESTIONS
   Q1_adaptive_beats_sbs_anywhere
      answer  : No.
      evidence: Primary configuration: 0 of 40 graph x Q cells have adaptive below SBS; the smallest loss is +0.378 ms (single graph, Q=500). With compiled-rule inference (a deployment variant, not the primary configuration) 4 of 40 cells are nominally below SBS, by at most 2.419 ms = 0.23% of the Q=500 SBS cost, i.e. far inside the 9% pass-to-pass dispersion of the same quantity; these are not reported as a crossover.
   Q2_why_not
      answer  : Two independent reasons, quantified separately.
      evidence: 
      solver_side: The selector's expected solver cost equals the SBS policy's: solver-only capture = -0.1633 of the oracle gap at Q=500 (chosen-solver total 1084.210 ms vs SBS 1064.200 ms).
      overhead_side: Required capture at Q=500 is 3.853 of the oracle gap with library per-query inference and 0.290 with compiled rules; the oracle gap is only 11.51% of the SBS cost.
   Q3_vbs_headroom
      answer  : Small but real.
      evidence: Oracle gap over 8 test graphs and 4,000 queries at Q=500: 122.523 ms = 11.51% of the SBS policy cost. VBS wins overall: Bidirectional Dijkstra takes most instances.
   Q4_capture
      answer  : No.
      evidence: CaptureRate at Q=500 = -4.0160; required = 3.8527. Solver-only (overhead-free) capture = -0.1633.
   Q5_capture_vs_Q
      answer  : Capture is roughly Q-independent and never positive at aggregate level.
      evidence: Q=1:-847.893 Q=10:-7.035 Q=50:-3.849 Q=100:-4.102 Q=500:-4.016
   Q6_required_capture_vs_Q
      answer  : Required capture falls with Q because the graph-level feature cost is paid once and the per-query costs are roughly constant while the cumulative oracle gap grows.
      evidence: Q=1:847.893 Q=10:6.847 Q=50:3.728 Q=100:3.940 Q=500:3.853
   Q7_tier_expansion
      answer  : No. Richer features cost more than they return.
      evidence: Analysis-grade required capture at Q=500: tier 0 = 2.828 (library) / 0.114 (compiled); exact accuracy is statistically indistinguishable across tiers.
   Q8_generalizes_to_unseen_configurations
      answer  : No.
      evidence: Primary tier 1: leave-one-cell-out median exact accuracy 80.80% vs majority 77.95% (median lift +0.00pp over 24 folds); the tree predicts a constant solver in 9 of those folds.
   Q9_generalizes_across_families
      answer  : No.
      evidence: Primary tier 1: leave-one-family-out median exact accuracy 79.09% vs majority 83.45% (median lift +0.00pp over 3 folds); the tree predicts a constant solver in 1 of those folds.
   Q1b_compiled_variant_nominal_wins
      answer  : Nominally, with compiled-rule inference, a small number of graph x Q cells sit a fraction of a percent below SBS; the margin is one to two orders of magnitude smaller than the pass-to-pass dispersion of the same quantity, so it is not a reproducible crossover.
      evidence: 4 of 40 cells below SBS, largest -2.419 ms; pass-to-pass dispersion of the same aggregate wall clock 9.4%.
   Q10_bottleneck
      answer  : Selector efficacy is the binding constraint whenever inference is cheap (compiled rules); per-query selection overhead is the binding constraint when inference goes through a general-purpose model library. Solver diversity is not the constraint.
      evidence: compiled: required 0.290 < 1.0, so even a perfect selector would pay, but achievable capture is -0.1633. library: required 3.853 > 1.0, so no selector could pay.

